  "e185d37f-7d00-4eb2-bc6c-a7ef2baa28a6": {
   "kind": "theory-definition",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p5-investigation-introduction",
     "replacement": "Consider a function $f(x)$.\nIf the inverse relation of $f$ is a function, it is the **inverse function** $f^{-1}(x)$, and it undoes $f$.\n\n$f^{-1}(x)$ is the inverse function and $(f(x))^{-1}$ is the reciprocal function $\\frac{1}{f(x)}$.\n\nFor the function $f(x)=2x$ that doubles every input,\nthe inverse function is $f^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x}{2}$, which halves every input.\n\n$\\sin^{-1}(x)$ is the inverse of $\\sin(x)$ when the domain of sine is restricted to $-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq x\\leq\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\nApplying a function and then its inverse returns the original input, for inputs in the appropriate domains.\n\n$$f^{-1}(f(x))=x,\\qquad f(f^{-1}(x))=x$$",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p5-investigation-introduction",
     "targetFingerprint": "5c682dbc04da324fa95c54ca879ab5fc43e2ae24e641f18ea63d914d33527471",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/inverse-functions-v1/20261111-025857",
   "revision": 306
  },
  "7599854a-b688-4c87-a553-6cc0ce130afd": {
   "kind": "theory-wording",
   "payloads": [
