  "e92244ae-15a5-428c-937e-37ee8588ed59": {
   "kind": "theory-definition",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p7-teaching",
     "replacement": "- The **inverse relation** of a function undoes what the function does.\n- The graph of a function and its inverse relation are **reflections across **$\\boldsymbol{y=x}$.\n- Find the inverse relation by **swapping the **$\\boldsymbol{x}$** and **$\\boldsymbol{y}$** variables**, then making $y$ the subject where possible.\n\n⚠\nThe inverse relation of a function is not always a function.\nWe will explore this further in a future lesson",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p7-teaching",
     "targetFingerprint": "fe4337038d69859919f98f33eef16b372101d3e95dc14441eb9b297f0335fbf9",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/inverse-functions-v1/20261111-025857",
   "revision": 306
  },
  "2e639aad-c2b4-4f2e-97ea-b55bb851e578": {
   "kind": "theory-definition",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p13-information",
     "replacement": "- The inverse of a function is not always a function itself.\n- Remember: if a relation passes the vertical line test, it is a function.\n- A function is **one-to-one** if each output comes from exactly one input, that is, if it passes the **horizontal line test**.\n- The inverse swaps the $x$- and $y$-coordinates, so the inverse of a function is a function exactly when the function is one-to-one.\n- **Even functions do not have an inverse function** on a symmetric domain containing nonzero inputs: symmetry means they fail the horizontal line test. A suitable domain restriction may give an inverse function.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p13-information",
     "targetFingerprint": "95401b2164fe0ab67ec8befaf6bcaa22a4538b9ec23654c6060d65744a1e6cb9",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
